 3. 💡 Historic demographic flip 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Census Bureau's International Database. Chart: Noah Bressner/Axios People 65 and older now outnumber children ages 5 and younger worldwide for the first time in recorded history, Axios' Russell Contreras writes from a new report by the Census Bureau. 

 Why it matters: A global plunge in fertility — demographers' term for the number of children being born — and increased longevity are creating a demographic inversion that's projected to deepen for decades. 🧮 By the numbers: More than 71% of the world's population lived in countries with fertility rates at or below the roughly 2.1-births-per-woman replacement level in 2023.

 That's up from 45% in 2013. The Census says the world reached that tipping point much earlier than demographers expected just a decade ago. The population of those 65+ is projected to grow from 852 million in 2025 to 2 billion in 2060. Keep reading ... Explore the data . 

 
 
